  completeExplanation: 'Chapter 2 of Ganita Prakash formalizes the grammar and structure of arithmetic expressions.\n\nThe chapter begins with simple expressions combining numbers with +, -, *, and ÷. Each expression evaluates to a single numerical value linked by the equality sign "=". Students compare expressions through structural reasoning rather than calculation (e.g. 1023 + 125 < 1022 + 128 because 128 adds 3 more while 1023 only adds 1 more; 113 - 25 = 112 - 24 because both minuend and subtrahend are decreased by 1).\n\nSection 2.2 addresses ambiguity in complex expressions (like 30 + 5 * 4) through an analogy with language punctuation. Brackets () define operational priority, commanding that internal operations be completed first.\n\nSection 2.3 introduces the foundational notion of "Terms". A term is defined as any part of an expression separated by a "+" sign. All subtractions are converted into additions of additive inverses: 83 - 14 = (83) + (-14). Multiplications and divisions bind tightly within single composite terms (e.g. 4 * 6 or 100 ÷ 2). The core evaluation rule is: evaluate each individual term first, then add the resulting term values.\n\nSection 2.4 applies the commutative (T1 + T2 = T2 + T1) and associative ((T1 + T2) + T3 = T1 + (T2 + T3)) properties of addition to terms. Because all inter-term operations are additions of integers, terms can be rearranged in any convenient order (such as grouping positive numbers together and negative numbers together).\n\nSection 2.5 details bracket removal. When brackets are preceded by a negative sign, every term inside changes its sign upon removal: a - (b + c) = a - b - c, and a - (b - c) = a - b + c. Subtracting a difference means subtracting b, but since c was subtracted too much, c must be added back.\n\nSection 2.6 establishes the Distributive Property of Multiplication over Addition (a(b + c) = ab + ac) and Subtraction (a(b - c) = ab - ac). "The multiple of a sum or difference equals the sum or difference of the multiples." This provides rapid mental math shortcuts near multiples of 10, 50, or 100 (e.g. 97 * 25 = (100 - 3) * 25 = 2500 - 75 = 2425).\n\nThe chapter concludes with multi-step real-world problems (window dimensions, Binu\'s annual savings, snail climb puzzle) and "Expression Engineer" puzzles.',
